The loader runs on the UAT server NLGTPTVAPP444 and the Production server NLGPPTVAPP447.

Before starting the data load, ensure the following are completed in Salesforce and on the loader server:

1. Install the Salesforce CLI (sf) on the UAT and Production servers.
2. Create an External Client App in Salesforce with the Client Credentials Flow enabled.
3. Record the Consumer Key and Consumer Secret from the External Client App.
4. Assign a run-as user to the Client Credentials Flow with the API Enabled permission.
5. Ensure the run-as user has read/write access to all objects and fields used by the loads.
6. Confirm every field used as an upsert key is flagged External ID or Unique on its object.
7. Confirm outbound HTTPS (443) access from the server to the Salesforce My Domain URL.
8. Place the bean file, SDL mapping files and clientcreds.json in the Config folder.

Note: The solution has been validated with Salesforce CLI v2.136.8 (win32-x64, Node v22.19.0). The Windows x64 installer bundles Node.js, so a separate Node installation is not required. Confirm the install on each server with sf --version.
point 7:
Test-NetConnection <your-salesforce-domain> -Port 443

Perform these steps on the UAT server (NLGTPTVAPP444) first, then repeat on the Production server (NLGPPTVAPP447).

Step 1 – Install the Salesforce CLI

Download and run the Salesforce CLI Windows x64 installer. Node.js is bundled with the installer, so a separate Node installation is not required.

Step 2 – Verify the installation

sf --version

Expected output:

@salesforce/cli/2.136.8 win32-x64 node-v22.19.0

Step 3 – Confirm curl.exe is available

where curl

curl.exe is used to request the OAuth token and is included with Windows Server 2019 and later.

Step 4 – Add the credentials file

Create <root>\Config\clientcreds.json with the External Client App details recorded earlier:

{
  "domain":       "<My Domain URL of the target org>",
  "clientId":     "<Consumer Key>",
  "clientSecret": "<Consumer Secret>",
  "org":          "<alias>"
}

This file replaces the stored username and password. It is the only file that differs between UAT and Production.

Step 5 – Remove the old authentication entries from the bean file

In <root>\Config\process-conf.xml, delete the following entries from every bean:

- sfdc.endpoint
- sfdc.username
- sfdc.password
- process.encryptionKeyFile

Leave all other entries unchanged. Each bean retains its object, operation, external ID field, mapping file and source file, so the load definitions and sequence are unaffected.

The Data Loader key file (dataLoader.key) is no longer used once these entries are removed, and can be retired in line with your key-handling policy.

▎ Note: No sf org login step is required. The load process requests its own token from the External Client App at the start of the run.
